# PHASE 3 — MATCH DATA CONSISTENCY AND VALIDATION

A second issue was observed during external testing.

One match result incorrectly included a note as both:

- A shared note.
- A missing or divergent note.

This is a data consistency defect.

Correct the matching result generation architecture.

## 3.1 — Canonical Note Identity

Use canonical note IDs to compare reference and user compositions.

Do not rely on raw display-name string comparison.

Normalize aliases and spelling variations without merging genuinely different materials.

## 3.2 — Derive All Result Lists From One Data Model

Shared notes, missing notes, additional notes, and tier differences must all be calculated from the same normalized comparison result.

Use logically defined sets.

For example:

- Shared Notes = User Notes ∩ Reference Notes.
- User-Only Notes = User Notes − Reference Notes.
- Reference-Only Notes = Reference Notes − User Notes.

A note must never belong to contradictory categories.

## 3.3 — Runtime Validation

Before displaying each match, validate:

- No duplicate notes in any category.
- No note appears simultaneously as shared and missing.
- Every shared note exists in both profiles.
- Every user-only note exists in the user's composition.
- Every reference-only note exists in the reference record.
- Pyramid tier assignments are consistent.
- Percentages and score values are valid.
- Missing reference data is not treated as a confirmed absence.

If validation fails, prevent the incorrect result from being presented and log the underlying issue.

Do not silently produce contradictory analysis.

